JUSTICECOMPASS EVALUATION METRICS
================================

Purpose
-------
This document defines the evaluation metrics for the JusticeCompass legal RAG
system. The system should be evaluated as four separate components:

1. Knowledge-base retrieval
2. Generated answer quality and grounding
3. Historical outcome estimation
4. System performance and reliability

Do not report the retrieval confidence score as a legal-success probability.
Retrieval confidence measures source relevance and quality. Outcome estimation
must be evaluated separately and must be reported as a historical pattern, not
a guarantee about an individual case.

Evaluation dataset
------------------
Create a held-out test set of questions that is not used to tune prompts,
retrieval thresholds, or models. Each question should have:

- The question text
- Legal domain and jurisdiction
- Relevant statute sections or authoritative sources
- Relevant case identifiers where applicable
- Required legal elements or facts
- Expected procedural next steps, reviewed by a legal expert
- Whether the available knowledge base is sufficient

For prediction evaluation, each historical case must also have a reviewed
outcome label, such as allowed, dismissed, rejected, granted, or settled. Split
prediction data by time where possible: train on earlier cases and test on
later cases. This better represents real-world use than a random split.

1. Retrieval metrics
--------------------
These metrics measure whether the system retrieves the correct legal sources.
They require a relevance label for each returned document.

Precision@K
Definition: proportion of the top K retrieved documents that are relevant.
Formula: Precision@K = relevant documents in top K / K
Use: Measures how much of the displayed context is useful.

Recall@K
Definition: proportion of all known relevant documents retrieved in the top K.
Formula: Recall@K = relevant documents in top K / all relevant documents
Use: Measures whether important authorities were missed.

Hit Rate@K
Definition: percentage of questions with at least one relevant document in
the top K results.
Formula: Hit Rate@K = questions with a relevant top-K result / total questions
Use: A simple measure of whether retrieval finds anything useful.

MRR (Mean Reciprocal Rank)
Definition: average reciprocal rank of the first relevant result.
Formula: MRR = average(1 / rank of first relevant result)
Use: Rewards placing the first useful authority near the top.

nDCG@K
Definition: ranking-quality metric that supports graded relevance labels, for
example 0 = irrelevant, 1 = related, 2 = directly applicable.
Use: Measures whether the most useful legal authorities are ranked first.

Recommended retrieval table columns:
- Collection: statutes, case law, cross-reference, or all
- K value
- Precision@K
- Recall@K
- Hit Rate@K
- MRR
- nDCG@K

2. Answer quality and grounding metrics
---------------------------------------
These metrics measure the generated response. Use expert review and, where
possible, an answer reference created by a qualified legal reviewer.

Citation precision
Definition: percentage of citations in the answer that are supported by the
retrieved source text.
Formula: supported citations / all cited sources

Citation recall
Definition: percentage of required authoritative sources that were cited.
Formula: required sources cited / all required sources

Citation completeness
Definition: percentage of material legal claims that have a supporting source.
Formula: supported material claims / all material claims

Faithfulness / groundedness
Definition: percentage of factual and legal claims that can be verified from
the retrieved context. Score claims individually and average them.

Answer relevance
Definition: whether the answer directly addresses the user's question,
without irrelevant or unsupported discussion. Use a 1-5 expert rating.

Actionability
Definition: whether the response provides usable next steps, documents to
collect, likely forum, missing facts, and deadline warnings where supported.
Use a 1-5 expert rating.

Legal safety
Definition: whether the answer avoids fabricated citations, guarantees,
unauthorized legal advice, and unsupported predictions. Report the percentage
of answers with no critical safety violation.

Structured-output validity
Definition: percentage of LLM responses that parse into the required JSON
schema without manual repair.
Formula: valid structured responses / total responses

Recommended expert-review scale:
- 1 = incorrect or unsafe
- 2 = mostly incorrect or incomplete
- 3 = partly useful, substantial omissions
- 4 = mostly correct and useful
- 5 = accurate, grounded, complete for the supplied facts

3. Historical outcome estimation metrics
----------------------------------------
Use these only after collecting enough reviewed outcome labels. Evaluate each
legal category separately; do not combine bail, consumer, family, property,
and criminal outcomes into one score.

Accuracy
Definition: fraction of outcome labels predicted correctly.
Formula: correct predictions / all predictions
Limitation: can be misleading when one outcome is much more common.

Precision, recall, and F1
Use per outcome class, especially when allowed and rejected cases are
imbalanced. Report macro-F1 in addition to micro-F1.

ROC-AUC or PR-AUC
Use only when the estimator produces a continuous score. PR-AUC is often more
informative for rare favorable outcomes.

Brier score
Definition: mean squared error between predicted probability and observed
binary outcome.
Formula: Brier = average((predicted probability - observed outcome)^2)
Lower is better.

Calibration error
Compare predicted probability buckets with observed favorable rates. Report
Expected Calibration Error (ECE) and include a reliability diagram.
A model should not display a percentage to users until calibration has been
checked on held-out data.

Coverage / abstention rate
Definition: percentage of questions for which the model provides an estimate
instead of returning insufficient_data.
Also report accuracy only on covered cases and the error rate of abstained cases.
A conservative system may have lower coverage but safer behavior.

Subgroup and stability checks
Report metrics separately by:
- Jurisdiction and court
- Legal domain and case type
- Year or time period
- Outcome class
- Data completeness level

For the current JusticeCompass implementation, the correct result when there
are too few clearly labeled live judgments is insufficient_data, not zero and
not an invented probability.

4. Operational metrics
----------------------
These metrics measure whether the system is usable.

- End-to-end latency: p50, p95, and p99 milliseconds
- Retrieval latency: p50 and p95 milliseconds
- LLM latency: p50 and p95 milliseconds
- Error rate: failed requests / total requests
- Timeout rate
- Cache hit rate
- Indian Kanoon API calls per assessment
- Average API cost per live assessment
- Number of retrieved context documents
- Token usage per LLM request

5. Results section template
---------------------------
Use wording similar to the following in the paper:

"JusticeCompass was evaluated using a held-out set of legal questions covering
multiple legal domains. Retrieval quality was measured using Precision@K,
Recall@K, Hit Rate@K, MRR, and nDCG@K. Generated responses were reviewed for
citation precision, citation completeness, groundedness, answer relevance,
actionability, legal safety, and structured-output validity. Historical outcome
estimation was evaluated separately using accuracy, macro-F1, Brier score,
calibration error, and coverage. Because the available case corpus is not
uniform across legal domains, results were reported by case type and
jurisdiction rather than as one aggregate legal-success score. The system
abstained when the comparable outcome sample was insufficient."

Do not claim measured values in the paper until the held-out evaluation has
actually been run. Report the number of questions, number of reviewers,
reviewer agreement, corpus date range, jurisdictions, model name, prompt
version, retrieval K, and confidence threshold alongside every result table.
